One should carefully check that the model indeed is a forward-confluent model, that is, both (LC) and (CR) are satisfied. We observe that only $\prec_h$-reflexivity of the points $(2,0)$ and $(2,1)$ is strictly needed to comply with (LC) and (CR). We made the other worlds $\prec_h$-reflexive too as to obtain a $\sf Grz.3$ model.

It is straightforward to verify that $\mathcal{M}_1, (0, 0) \not \Vdash \varphi$ and using the assumption that $\ensuremath{\text{ 


  \textit{\fontfamily{qzc}\selectfont3} 
}^{*}}\models \mathsf L_h$ and $\ensuremath{\text{ 


  \textit{\fontfamily{qzc}\selectfont2} 
}^{\Ydown}}\models \mathsf L_v$, the model $\mathcal M_1$ is a $\mathsf L_h\rtimes \mathsf L_v$-model. By Proposition \ref{SoundFor-Conflu}, we obtain $\not\vdash_{[{\sf L}_h, {\sf L}_v]^{\sf e}} \varphi$ for ${\sf L}_h$ and ${\sf L}_v$ as in the statement of this theorem. Since $\varphi$ holds on any  $(\mathsf L_h\times \mathsf L_v)^{\sf e}$-model, we conclude the incompleteness result.

Let $\mathsf L_h \supseteq \sf Grz$ be valid on $\ensuremath{\text{ 


  \textit{\fontfamily{qzc}\selectfont2} 
}^{*}}$ and ${\sf L}_v$ be any logic that is valid on $\ensuremath{\text{ 


  \textit{\fontfamily{qzc}\selectfont2} 
}^{\Ydown}}$.
Then, the logic $[\mathsf L_h , \mathsf L_v]^{\sf e}$ is incomplete for its expanding domain semantics. 

In particular, all of $[{\sf{Grz}} , {\sf K}]^{\sf e}$,
$[{\sf Grz} , {\sf K4}]^{\sf e}$, and
$[{\sf Grz} , {\sf GL}]^{\sf e}$ are incomplete for their expanding domain semantics.

We will see that
\[
\psi \ := \ p\wedge \Box_h (\boxdot_v  p\vee \boxdot_v \neg p )  \to \Diamond_h (p\wedge \Box_v (q\to \Box_h (p\to q))) 
\]
is valid over $ (\sf Grz \times K)^{\sf e}$.
To this end, we consider an arbitrary $ (\sf Grz \times K)^{\sf e}$-model $\mathfrak M$ and some $(x,x')$ with $\mathfrak{M}, (x,x')\Vdash p\wedge \Box_h (\boxdot_v  p\vee \boxdot_v \neg p )$.
Since the horizontal accessibility is reflexive, we will write it as $\preccurlyeq_h$ and reserve $\prec_h$ for its irreflexive part, i.e. $x\prec_h y$ if $x\preccurlyeq_h y$ but $y\not\preccurlyeq_h x$.

Since the horizontal frame is Noetherian and transitive, there is $y$ with $x \preccurlyeq _hy$ so that 
$(y,x')\Vdash \boxdot_v p$ but, whenever $(y,x')\prec_h (z,x')$, we have that $(z,x')\Vdash \boxdot_v \neg p$. It is now easy to see that $(y,x')\Vdash \Box_v (q\to \Box_h (p\to q))$: if $x' \prec_v^yu$, by the design of $\sf e$-frames, any strict $\prec_h$ successor of $(y,u)$ cannot validate $p$ and in particular validates $p\to q$. If $(y,u)\nVdash q$, then trivially $(y,u)\Vdash q \to \Box_h (p \to q)$ and if $(y,u)\Vdash q$, then $(y,u)\Vdash p\to q$ whence $(y,u)\Vdash \Box_h (p\to q)$ and $(y,u)\Vdash q \to \Box_h (p\to q)$. We conclude $(y,x')\Vdash p\wedge \Box_v (q\to \Box_h (p\to q))$ whence $(x,x') \Vdash p\wedge \Box_h (\boxdot_v  p\vee \boxdot_v \neg p ) \to \Diamond_h (p\wedge \Box_v (q\to \Box_h (p\to q)))$ as was to be shown.

With $\mathsf L_h$ and $\mathsf L_v$ being as in the statement of the theorem, we now consider the $\mathsf L_h\rtimes \mathsf L_v$-model $\mathcal{M}_2 = (W,\preccurlyeq_h,\prec_v,\left\llbracket \cdot \right\rrbracket)$ as depicted in Figure \ref{fig2}.

